---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import BrandMark from '../components/BrandMark.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Bookmarklet — Starisk"
  description="Put the Starisk score next to Coursera's own rating, on Coursera's pages. A bookmarklet, not an extension."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
    <header>
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-base-content/50">Tools</p>
      <h1 class="mt-1 font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">
        The Starisk bookmarklet
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/75">
        The score next to the score, on Coursera's own pages. No extension, no account, no
        permissions prompt — a bookmark you click.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8 rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200/50 p-6">
      <h2 class="font-display text-lg font-bold">Install it once</h2>

      <ol class="mt-5 space-y-5 text-sm">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span
            class="flex h-6 w-6 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-neutral text-xs font-semibold text-neutral-content"
            >1</span
          >
          <span>
            Show your bookmarks bar: <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">⌘</kbd> <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">⇧</kbd>
            <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">B</kbd> on macOS, <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd
              class="kbd kbd-xs">Shift</kbd
            >+<kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">B</kbd> elsewhere.
          </span>
        </li>

        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span
            class="flex h-6 w-6 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-neutral text-xs font-semibold text-neutral-content"
            >2</span
          >
          <div class="flex-1">
            <p>Drag this button onto the bar.</p>
            <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
              <a id="bookmarklet" class="btn btn-primary gap-2" href="#">
                <BrandMark class="h-4 w-4" /> Starisk
              </a>
              <button id="copy" class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm" type="button">Copy the link</button>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/60">
              No dragging? Right-click the button → <em>Bookmark this link…</em> in Chrome or Edge,
              <em>Bookmark This Link</em> in Firefox. The copied link also works pasted into a new
              bookmark's <em>address</em> field.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span
            class="flex h-6 w-6 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-neutral text-xs font-semibold text-neutral-content"
            >3</span
          >
          <span>
            Open a Coursera course or search page and click the bookmark. It runs only when you
            click it, and only on the page in front of you.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-5 border-t border-base-300 pt-4 text-xs text-base-content/60">
        Dragging from this page is also what gives the bookmark its icon. Creating it by pasting a
        URL leaves the generic one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <details class="mt-6 rounded-box border border-base-300 p-4">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm font-medium">It did not work</summary>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-base-content/70">
        <li>
          <strong>The browser went to this page instead.</strong> The bookmark saved the page address
          rather than the link. Edit it: the address must start with <code>javascript:</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing appeared.</strong> There is only something to draw on a course, program or
          search page — not on Coursera's homepage, and not on other sites.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It vanished after a click.</strong> That is expected: a bookmarklet does not
          persist. Click it again on the next page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Phone or tablet.</strong> No mobile browser runs a bookmarklet, iOS Safari included.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">What it does</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-base-content/80">
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            On a course page it opens a small panel with Coursera's headline rating, the score
            Starisk computes, and the gap between them.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            On a search or catalog page it adds a chip to every result we have read, and one line
            with the honest count: <em>Starisk has read 5 of these 12</em>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            On a course we have not read yet, it offers a button that asks for it. The request
            lands in the same queue as the one on this site.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Where it works</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-hidden rounded-box border border-base-300">
        <table class="table table-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Page</th>
              <th>What appears</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono text-xs">coursera.org/learn/…</td>
              <td>Panel with both scores, the gap and the review count</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono text-xs">…/specializations/…<br />…/professional-certificates/…</td>
              <td>Panel with the average of the courses we read</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono text-xs">coursera.org/search?query=…</td>
              <td>A chip on each known result, plus the count line</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono text-xs">coursera.org/courses?query=…</td>
              <td>The same, for programs</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">What to know</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-base-content/80">
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            Desktop only. iOS Safari cannot run a bookmarklet, and no mobile browser will.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            It reads our data and writes nothing to Coursera. The only request it makes is for{' '}
            <a class="link" href="/data/badge.json">/data/badge.json</a> — 24 KB gzipped.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            If Coursera changes its markup, the badge stops appearing. It does not break the page:
            every step is wrapped, and nothing is styled globally.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <details class="mt-10 rounded-box border border-base-300 p-4">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm font-medium">Read the source</summary>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
        This is what the link contains — readable, unminified, and with no network calls other
        than the one above.
      </p>
      <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-field bg-base-200 p-4 text-xs leading-relaxed"><code
          data-bookmarklet-source></code
        ></pre>
    </details>
  </article>

  <script>
    import { bookmarkletSource } from '../lib/bookmarklet.js';

    // Built from this page's own origin, so the bookmark points at whatever host
    // you are reading it on — localhost while testing, the public domain in
    // production. The alternative, a hardcoded origin, is a value someone has to
    // remember to change back before committing.
    const source = bookmarkletSource(location.origin);
    const code = `javascript:${encodeURIComponent(source)}`;

    const link = document.getElementById('bookmarklet');
    const copy = document.getElementById('copy');
    const listing = document.querySelector('[data-bookmarklet-source]');

    link.href = code;
    if (listing) listing.textContent = source;

    copy.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(code);
        copy.textContent = 'Copied';
      } catch {
        copy.textContent = 'Copy blocked — drag it instead';
      }
      setTimeout(() => {
        copy.textContent = 'Copy the link';
      }, 2500);
    });
  </script>
</Base>
